# Scroll degradation: the low-res / checkerboard tile ladder and its cost-based
# trigger, end to end.
# Merged from 9 patches: webkit-directional-tile-coverage-env,
# webkit-checkerboard-during-scroll-env, webkit-lowres-tiles-during-scroll-env,
# webkit-scrolltier-log-diagnostic, webkit-lowres-tiles-cpu-path,
# webkit-tile-raster-cost-instrumentation, webkit-lowres-cost-trigger,
# webkit-lowres-tile-edge-seam, webkit-lowres-sharpen-viewport-scope.
#
# These nine were an ordering chain over the same four files
# (CoordinatedBackingStoreProxy.{cpp,h}, CoordinatedBackingStoreTile.cpp,
# SkiaPaintingEngine.cpp) and only ever made sense applied together.
#
# What it does, in layers:
#  1. Directional tile coverage — bias the prepaint cushion along the scroll
#     direction instead of symmetrically (WEBKIT_DIRECTIONAL_TILE_COVERAGE,
#     WEBKIT_COVER_AREA_MULTIPLIER).
#  2. Low-res tiles during scroll — rasterize newly-exposed tiles at
#     WEBKIT_LOWRES_TILE_SCALE and bilinear-upscale at composite (Chrome/Android
#     "low-res tiles"), then repaint full-res at rest. Raster cost is quadratic
#     in scale, so this is the main lever on the serialized compositor-thread
#     paint path. Ships at 0.3; >= 1.0 disables.
#  3. Checkerboard tier — above WEBKIT_CHECKERBOARD_DURING_SCROLL, skip
#     rasterizing newly-exposed tiles entirely (page background, like Gecko APZ)
#     and defer to the settle timer (WEBKIT_CHECKERBOARD_SETTLE_MS).
#     Speed ladder: WEBKIT_LOWRES_SCROLL_SPEED (T1) < CHECKERBOARD (T2).
#  4. CPU-path fix — WEBKIT_SKIA_ENABLE_CPU_RENDERING=1 had silently disabled
#     the whole feature via the force-1.0 guards in paint/replay; the whole-tile
#     low-res buffer now uploads 1:1 into a buffer-sized texture.
#  5. Raster-cost instrumentation — per-painted-pixel cost EWMA sampled on the
#     real raster threads, exposed via SkiaPaintingEngine::tileRasterNsPerPixel().
#     Measures only. WEBKIT_TILECOST_LOG=1 to trace.
#  6. Cost trigger — decide degradation from PREDICTED RASTER COST vs the frame
#     budget instead of scroll speed, scale derived as 1/sqrt(overshoot), with
#     asymmetric hysteresis (engage on 1 over-budget pass, disengage after 4
#     under). Speed cannot discriminate: the same knob measured -42% p95 on a
#     raster-bound page and +7% p95 / +11% raster CPU on a cheap one, while
#     velocities were identical and raster cost separated them 23.7x.
#     WEBKIT_LOWRES_COST_TRIGGER, with _BUDGET_MS / _ENGAGE_X / _CHECKERBOARD_X /
#     _MOTION_MS / _DISENGAGE_PASSES / _SCALE_FLOOR.
#  7. Tile-edge seam fix — lowResBufferSize() ceil()'d the buffer while content
#     painted at the requested scale, leaving a transparent fractional texel
#     column that upscaled into a dark grid at every tile edge. Size with
#     lround() and paint at bufferSize/fullSize so paint and composite ratios
#     are exact inverses. Correctness fix, not gated; affects the speed ladder
#     too (it was masked there by the sharpen-at-rest repaint).
#  8. Viewport-scoped sharpen — the sharpen-at-rest pass repainted EVERY low-res
#     tile full-res including the prepaint cushion (cover 2 => ~half were never
#     looked at), landing just as the user stops scrolling; that fixed
#     per-ENGAGEMENT cost is why halving engagement changed the cheap-page
#     penalty by nothing. WEBKIT_LOWRES_SHARPEN_VIEWPORT_ONLY limits it to the
#     viewport + WEBKIT_LOWRES_SHARPEN_MARGIN_PX (512); cushion tiles sharpen
#     when the viewport reaches them.
#  9. WEBKIT_SCROLLTIER_LOG=1 traces the whole ladder (dy-path eligibility,
#     arming decisions, the 0 < dt < 0.2 gate). Later layers depend on its
#     helper, which is why the diagnostic lives here rather than separately.
#
# Shipped defaults live in deploy/runtime-common.sh — check there, not here, for
# what a build actually runs. Must apply AFTER webkit-raster-on-compositor-thread-env
# and webkit-skia-record-rtree-env (same SkiaPaintingEngine.cpp contexts).
#
# ---
